# Chapter 1, part 4: the tools (programs of the package) and the help.
.h2 The programs and the Help [view-tools]
.ix Tools
.ix Programs of Strata
.ix Package, programs
.ix Editor
.ix Document Manager
.ix Printer
.ix Viewer
.ix keycaps
Strata is a set of programs that work together.  The Document Manager and the
Editor are the programs that a user starts; the others work out of sight when
a document is printed or previewed.  This section says what each program is
for, how it is reached and what it needs.  {n:appcmd} describes the
environment settings and the files.

.table 12 22 25
Tool | What it is | How you reach it
The Editor | The program that edits | Edit / Preview / Print Document
 | documents. | of the Document Manager.
The Document Manager | The menu that starts the | The usual way to start
 | Editor and keeps the | Strata.
 | shared settings. |
The Printer | Makes a PDF (or a DVI) from | Print, Draft or Final in the
 | a document, through LaTeX. | Editor.
The Viewer | Shows the pages of a DVI | Print, Preview in the Editor.
 | file. |
Keycaps | A marker and a style of | Marker, Keycap; and the keys
 | picture: a key drawn as a key. | in this manual.
Help | The topics of a help file, | {k:F1} in the Editor and in
 | shown by the Editor and the | the Document Manager.
 | Document Manager. |
.end

.h3 The Editor [view-stred]
.ix Editor
.ix Starting the Editor
The Editor edits documents.  It reads and writes the Strata document format:
the files with the suffix {c:.doc}, the templates {c:.set}, the libraries
{c:.lib} and the macro files {c:.mac}, which are the same kind of file.  A
document that is loaded and saved without a change is the same file.  The
Editor runs in a terminal or in an X11 window, according to the version that
was built.

Started with no file, the Editor opens its own small Document Manager over an
empty screen ({n:view-docmgr-inside}).
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.ix Options of the Editor
The Editor takes options and one document name; {x:appcmd-stred-opt} lists
the options.  With a file name that does not exist, the Editor opens a new
empty document that is saved under that name; with a name that exists but
is not a document, it ends with the reason.
.endif

.h3 The Document Manager [view-str]
.ix Document Manager
.ix Master menu
.ix Starting Strata
The Document Manager is the first screen when Strata is started in the usual
way.  It is a menu that starts the other programs and keeps the settings that
they share.

.pic pictures/view-str.png 5.0 The Document Manager (X11 version).

.table 26 34
Entry | What it does
Edit / Preview / Print Document | Asks for a document, then starts the Editor
 | on it; Print, Preview and Spell are on the menus of the Editor.
Maintain Dictionaries | Edits the words of the user that the spell check
 | accepts.
Compare Document Versions | Shows what is different between two documents,
 | such as a document and its backup.
Restore Backup | Brings back one of the backups of a document.
TeX Font Association | Chooses the TeX font for each font of the list.
Setup | The working directory, the Editor program, the help
 | file, Autosave, the printer and the language of the
 | spell check.
Quit | Leaves the Document Manager.
.end
The keys are those of a list of choices ({n:view-list}) with these
differences: {k:Ins} chooses as {k:Enter} does, {k:F1} shows the help for the
entry, and {k:Esc} at the foot says cancel.  {n:str} describes every
entry, every panel and every field.  The Document Manager takes the option
{c:-H} (the help file) and the name of a document; {n:appcmd-str-opt} lists
the options.

.h4 The small Document Manager of the Editor [view-docmgr-inside]
.ix Document Manager inside the Editor
.ix Editor with no file
The Editor, started with no file, draws the same kind of list over an empty
screen (the status line shows {c:(new)}).  It has four entries: Edit / Preview /
Print Document, which offers Enter a file name, Choose from this directory
and New document; Maintain Dictionaries and Compare Document Versions, which
open the same panels as the entries of the Document Manager of the same
names; and Quit, which leaves the Editor.  To check the spelling of a
document, open it and use Tools, Spell.

.pic pictures/view-docmgr.png 5.0 The Editor started with no file.

.h3 The Printer [view-strprint]
.ix Printer
.ix Printing, the Printer
.ix PDF
.ix LaTeX
.ix DVI
The Printer turns a document into a PDF through LaTeX.  The Print and Preview
commands of the Editor start it.  The Printer needs {c:pdflatex} (or {c:latex})
and the packages that the document uses; a missing program is reported by name.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.ix Options of the Printer
The Printer takes the name of a document and options; {x:appcmd-strprint-opt}
lists the options.  With no document, or with an option that is not valid, the
Printer shows its usage and ends with status 2; a document that
cannot be read ends it with status 1.  {x:print-cmdline} describes the
command line.
.endif


.h3 The Viewer [view-dviview]
.ix Viewer
.ix Preview, Viewer
.ix DVI viewer
.ix Zoom, Viewer
.ix Go to page, Viewer
The Viewer shows the pages of a Preview, one page at a time, in an X11
window.  The Editor starts it for Print, Preview: the Printer makes the pages
(the Editor shows a wait panel and {k:Esc} stops it), and the Viewer opens
them.  The Viewer needs an X display; without {c:DISPLAY} the Editor reports
{c:Preview needs an X display: DISPLAY is not set.}  The title of the window is
the name
of the file.  At the bottom is a status bar, {c:Page 1 of 2   Zoom 100%}, and
a line of keys.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
The Viewer takes the name of the DVI file and options; {x:appcmd-dviview-opt}
lists the options.
.endif

.table 22 38
Key | Effect
{k:PgDn}, {k:Space}, {k:N} | The next page.
{k:PgUp}, {k:P} | The previous page.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The first page, the last page.
{k:G} | Go to a page: type its number, then {k:Enter}; {k:Esc} cancels.
{k:Up}, {k:Down}, {k:Left}, {k:Right} | Scroll the page.
{k:Keypad +}, {k:+}, {k:=} | Zoom in.
{k:Keypad -}, {k:-} | Zoom out.
{k:Keypad *}, {k:0}, {k:Keypad 0} | Back to 100 percent.
{k:F1}, {k:?} | Show or hide the line of keys.
{k:Q}, {k:Esc} | Close the Viewer.
.end
{n:print} describes the preview in full.

.pic pictures/view-dviview.png 5.0 A page in the Viewer.

.h3 Keycaps [view-keycaps]
.ix Keycaps
.ix Keycap marker
.ix Key, drawn as a key
.ix Legend (keycap)
A keycap is a key drawn as a key: a word or a sign inside a box with rounded
corners, set in the line of text.  The keys in this manual are keycaps.  The
marker command Keycap ({k:Alt-F12}, Keycap) inserts one; its panel has the
fields Legend (the text on the key, such as {c:PrtScr}), Foreground,
Background (shades from 0.0 to 100.0), Scale and Font.  A keycap is a marker
that behaves like a word: it sits in the line and moves with the text.  The
global values of the shades, the scale and the font are on the Marker panel
of the Global menu.

In the X11 version the keycap is drawn as a picture when its display mode is
Inline; when the picture cannot be made, and in the other modes, it is drawn
by the mode (in the Short mode as {c:[Key: legend]}).  The terminal version
draws the legend in reverse video with a space on each side.  Printed, the
keycap is the picture.  {x:view-screen-text} shows the keycaps of a document.
The Keycap marker is described in {n:markers}.

.h3 Help [view-help]
.ix Help
.ix F1
.ix Help file
.ix Help topics
.ix Hypertext links, help
.ix Help Index
.ix STRATA_HELP
{k:F1} shows the help for the place where the cursor is.  The help is written
in topics, and the topic shown is the one that fits: the highlighted entry of
the menu bar, the panel or the field that holds the cursor, the table or list
that is open, the marker under the cursor, the text editor of a note, a
comment or an equation, or the message that was on the message line when the
key was pressed.  At the edit screen with nothing else open the topic is Edit
Screen.  When there is no topic for the place, the topic of the screen is
shown.  {k:F1} works at the edit screen, in the menu bar, in every panel and
list, in the text editors of the markers, while a highlight is on, and in the
Document Manager.

The help also has topics for the equations: the symbols that can
be used, and what the messages mean when an equation cannot be typeset.

.pic pictures/view-help.png 5.4 The help topic Edit Screen.

The help is a window across the foot of the screen; the document stays in view
above it.  The first line of the window names the topic after {c:HELP:}, the
last line shows the keys, and between them is the text, broken to the width of
the window.  Keys in the text are drawn as keycaps, and the names of other
topics are links, drawn in bold and underlined; the chosen link is in reverse
video.

.table 24 36
Key | Effect
{k:Up}, {k:Down} | Scroll the topic by one line.
{k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn}, {k:Space} | Scroll by a page.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The start and the end of the topic.
{k:Tab}, {k:Right} | Choose the next link on the topic.
{k:Shift-Tab}, {k:Left} | Choose the previous link.
{k:Enter} | Follow the chosen link: show that topic.
{k:Backspace} | Go back to the previous topic.  With nothing to go back
 | to, close the help.
{k:I} | The index: a list of all the topics in the order of the alphabet;
 | choose one with {k:Enter}.
{k:Z} | Make the window as large as the screen, and back to the window at the
 | foot.
{k:Esc}, {k:F1}, {k:Q} | Close the help and return to the edit screen.
.end
{k:F1} in a panel shows the topic of the field that holds the cursor when
there is one, and the topic of the panel when not.  Closing the help leaves
the document as it was.

.h4 Finding the help file [view-help-file]
.ix Help file, search
.ix --help-file
The program uses the first help file that it can read: the file named by
{c:-H} (or {c:--help-file}); the file named by the environment variable
{c:STRATA_HELP}; the file named by the setting Help File of Setup in the
Document Manager; the file {c:strata.hlp} next to the programs, or in the
directory above them; and the directories {c:/usr/local/share/strata} and
{c:/usr/share/strata}.  If none is found, the Editor starts without help, and
{k:F1} shows a notice that reports this.  Setup can store the name of the file
for
the next time.
